{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And the land became defiled, and I would punish its iniquity upon it, etc.</b> If its inhabitants sinned, what wrong did the land commit that He should say, 'and I would punish its iniquity upon it'? And it appears that all of this follows from the earlier sin, for HaShem said, ״תּוֹצֵא הָאָרֶץ עֵץ פְּרִי״ — that the taste of the tree should be like the taste of the fruit — and she did not do so; therefore when man sinned, she too was called to account for her iniquity and was cursed. And one may ask: why was she not cursed immediately? Rather, because the essence of the change was that HaShem wanted her to give pure and refined matter to all existent things, and this matter touches upon all existent things whose origin is from the earth — and she did not do so, but gave coarse and thick matter in all of them. And this matter caused the taste of the tree not to be like the taste of the fruit, for the matter was coarse and turbid; and this caused also in man a yearning for corporeal lusts to an extreme degree, and had his matter been pure and clean he would not have come to the point of sin. Therefore the earth was not cursed until man sinned, for then the damage was more apparent; but in the matter of the tree yielding fruit, the damage was not so great." "And the Ramban wrote in parashat בְּרֵאשִׁית (Genesis 2:9) that the fruit of the tree of knowledge engendered desire for the act of union, and therefore they covered their nakedness after they ate of it. Therefore we find that in every place where the sin of forbidden sexual relations is prevalent, there the Holy One, blessed be He, punishes the iniquity of the land, for she caused the first man to sin with the tree of knowledge, and through this he acquired a great inclination toward forbidden relations. For in the generation of the Flood, where promiscuity was prevalent, it is said (Genesis 6:13): ״וְהִנְנִי מַשְׁחִיתָם אֶת הָאָרֶץ״ — together with the earth, for even three handbreadths of the plowshare were corrupted, and this was for the land by way of punishment, to purify it through boiling waters. And for this reason the land of Israel is called ״אֶרֶץ לֹא מְטֹהָרָה״, because it was not given rain on the day of wrath, as is found in Ezekiel (22:24), and it is written (Leviticus 26:32): ״וַהֲשִׁמּוֹתִי אֲנִי אֶת הָאָרֶץ״. And from here is a proof that at the time of wrath sin causes the earth to be corrupted, as it is written (Deuteronomy 29:21–22): ״וְרָאוּ אֶת מַכּוֹת הָאָרֶץ וְאֶת תַּחֲלֻאֶיהָ אֲשֶׁר חִלָּה ה׳ בָּהּ, גָּפְרִית וָמֶלַח שְׂרֵפָה כָל אַרְצָהּ״ — for through boiling waters it was corrupted, and through boiling waters it was judged with the burning of all its land. And likewise in the generation of the Flood it is written (Genesis 6:5): ״וַיַּרְא ה׳ כִּי רַבָּה רָעַת הָאָדָם בָּאָרֶץ״ — that the earth was affected by the wickedness of those who dwelt upon it." "And what is written, <b>״תַּחֲלֻאֶיהָ אֲשֶׁר חִלָּה ה׳ בָּהּ״</b> — one may say by way of parable that every sick person's way is to vomit what is within him; so too it is said here, in the parashah of forbidden relations, ״וַתָּקִיא הָאָרֶץ אֶת יוֹשְׁבֶיהָ״ — for it would be like the resemblance of a sick person who vomits the food that is within him, and of this it is said: ״וַתִּטְמָא הָאָרֶץ וָאֶפְקֹד עֲוֹנָהּ עָלֶיהָ״. And this is a precious interpretation."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:25.667305408Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}